{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1787138121,"nonce":"8i3G1odMcxAFbJV1FskLKjRHXJw=","target":"b04a2f300a911dde4a4646472b6aea3c7b3d60ed058e10e53fd4f25918ccc134","message":"**The demonstration.** `Decide` is the ordering gate: the one function that decides whether a queued delivery may send. Its state switch has no `default`, and since migration `00066` narrowed the thread vocabulary there is nothing else standing behind it.\n\n`internal/platform/jobs/ordering/ordering.go:181-189`:\n\n```go\nswitch th.State {\ncase StateDead:\n    return Decision{Action: ActionRecoverThread, Reason: \"thread_dead\"}\ncase StateOpening, StateOpen:\n}\n// ...falls through to the sequence logic\n```\n\n`StateOpening, StateOpen` is an empty arm, and there is no `default`. Every state that is not `dead` therefore reaches the sequence logic and, for a head-of-line item, returns `proceed` / `in_order`. A red-team pass fed it `\"frozen\"`, `\"\"`, `\"OPEN\"` and `\"garbage\"`: **all four returned `proceed`.**\n\nThree facts make that reachable rather than theoretical, and each is a grep:\n\n1. **The conversion is unchecked.** `internal/notification/repository/threads.go:363` does `State: ordering.ThreadState(state)` — a raw string conversion straight off the column, with no validation. `:46` does the same for `domain.ThreadState`.\n2. **`Valid()` exists and NOTHING calls it.** `internal/notification/domain/thread.go` defines `ThreadState.Valid()` as the closed-set check. `git grep '\\.Valid()'` over the whole tree returns **zero** hits for it. The guard was written and never wired — the same defect class as `e5c060b`'s `Freeze`.\n3. **The database can hold a value the binary does not know.** `00066`'s Down deliberately re-admits `frozen`, and `TestEveryMigrationDownTo00028IsReversible` now asserts that it does. So \"rolled-back schema, new binary\" is a state the test suite actively guarantees is constructible.\n\n**Why it matters.** The failure is silent and it is in the direction that sends. `dead` is tested first precisely because ADR 0023 established that a terminal state must decide NOW and never snooze; an unknown state is the one case where the gate cannot know whether it is terminal, and it currently resolves that by sending. Before `00066` a `frozen` row at least hit an explicit `abandon` arm. Now it posts.\n\n⚠️ **This is PRE-EXISTING and was not introduced by `00066`.** The switch never had a `default`; the vocabulary narrowing only made the hole easier to reach and easier to see. It is filed as its own ticket rather than folded into `e5c060b` because the fix is not a line — see Scope.\n\n**Where.** `internal/platform/jobs/ordering/ordering.go:181-189` (the switch) · `:20-25` (the vocabulary it switches over) · `internal/notification/repository/threads.go:46,363` (the two unchecked conversions) · `internal/notification/domain/thread.go` (`Valid()`, uncalled) · `db/migrations/00066_a_thread_is_never_frozen.sql` (whose Down re-admits `frozen`) · `docs/runbooks/oto_thread_order_decisions_total.md` and SPEC §G.7's verdict table (both of which declare the verdict vocabulary CLOSED).\n\n**Scope.** medium, and the size is all in the vocabulary rather than the code. The one-line fix — a `default` returning `abandon` — needs a `reason` label, and `oto_thread_order_decisions_total{action,reason}` is a declared-closed vocabulary: a new `unknown_state` reason has to be added to the runbook table and to SPEC §G.7's verdict table in the same change, which is the rule `00060` and the runbook both restate. There is also a real choice to make: `abandon` (refuse to send, record `skipped`) versus `recover_thread` (treat unknown as terminal and make the caller decide now, which is what ADR 0023 argues for terminal states). **`abandon` is the safer default and `recover_thread` is the more consistent one**; that is the decision this ticket needs, and it is not mine to take.\n\n**Done when.** `Decide` cannot return `proceed` for a state it does not recognise; a test enumerates at least `\"\"`, `\"garbage\"` and `\"frozen\"` and asserts the chosen verdict for each; the new verdict is in the runbook table and SPEC §G.7 alongside the existing eight; and `ThreadState.Valid()` is either wired into the two conversions at `threads.go:46,363` or deleted — an unwired closed-set guard is exactly the defect `e5c060b` was filed about, and leaving it is the third instance this branch has found.","files":null}]}